.TH APL 1
.SH NAME
apl \- APL interpreter with real right-to-left evaluation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B apl
.I file.apl
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Apl
is a work-alike of APL, the third language named in Nelson's
.IR "Computer Lib/Dream Machines" ,
and the one with the least in common with BASIC or TRAC: every value
is an array (a scalar is just a one-element array here), a small set
of primitives apply to a whole array at once, and \(em the single most
notorious thing about the language \(em expressions have no operator
precedence at all and evaluate strictly right to left.
.B "2×3+4"
is
.B "2×(3+4)=14,"
not
.B "(2×3)+4=10,"
because
.B 3+4
is further right and is applied first.
.PP
Real APL glyphs are used throughout, not ASCII substitutes: ←
(assign), ⍴ (shape/reshape), ⍳ (iota), ⌈ and ⌊
(ceiling and floor), ≤ and ≥ (comparisons), × and ÷
(multiply/divide), ⍝ (a comment glyph), and ¯ (a distinct
negative-literal prefix, never fused into the
.B \-
subtraction/negate function).
.PP
Primitives:
.B "+ - × ÷ ⌈ ⌊"
(monadic and dyadic each \(em identity/negate/sign/reciprocal/
ceiling/floor monadic, elementwise arithmetic dyadic with scalar
extension),
.B "= < > ≤ ≥"
and not-equal
(dyadic, elementwise 1/0),
.B ⍳
(monadic iota, index origin 1),
.B ⍴
(monadic shape, dyadic reshape \(em cycling through the right
argument), and reduce as a suffix on any of
.B "+ - × ÷ ⌈ ⌊"
.RB ( +/V ", " -/V ", etc.). "
Reduce folds from the right, same as real APL.
.PP
Not implemented, a deliberate scope cut: bracket indexing
.RB ( V[I] ),
rank beyond 1 (vectors and scalars only), and user-defined functions.
.SH FILES
.TF /lib/apl/tempconvert.apl
.TP
.B /lib/apl/basics.apl
Index generator, reshape, and the precedence-defying
.B "2×3+4"
itself.
.TP
.B /lib/apl/reduce.apl
Reduction and elementwise comparison, including a right-fold
subtraction.
.TP
.B /lib/apl/tempconvert.apl
A vector of Fahrenheit temperatures converted to Celsius all at once,
via scalar-extension broadcasting.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/apl.b
